+A second exact fixture separates row exchangeability from subgroup
+randomization.  Let the null put probability `1/2` on each of
+
+\[
+z^{(1)}=(10,0,-100,-100),\qquad
+z^{(2)}=(0,10,-100,-100).
+\]
+
+This law is invariant under the two-element group that swaps rows 0 and 1.
+Nevertheless, ranking row 0 against all four rows gives
+
+\[
+p_{\rm all}(z^{(1)})=\frac14,\qquad
+p_{\rm all}(z^{(2)})=\frac12,
+\]
+
+so
+
+\[
+\Pr(p_{\rm all}\le\tfrac12)=1>\frac12.
+\]
+
+The group-orbit rank uses only the two transformed statistics and gives
+\(p_{\mathcal G}=1/2\) or \(1\), which is super-uniform.  Thus a
+proper-subgroup invariance hypothesis does not validate the all-row rank; the
+reference set must be the actual null orbit or satisfy the stronger joint
+exchangeability theorem.
 
